// A very explicit range syntax would be:
// a<..<b
// a<..=b
// a=..<b
// a=..=b
// But this is kind of annoying to type, which I think is a real problem.
//
// I could make .. equivalent to =..<, but still allow the other options. This
// would at least make sure all four kinds are available, but it does still
// feel a bit underspecified, almost.
//
// Another option could be to have parens in the mix somehow...
// (a<<b)
// (a<=b)
// (a=<b)
// (a==b)
// Obviously this doesn't quite work.
//
// I think I would like to avoid using (], [) etc, because although they are
// unambiguous, they are a bit annoying to type (even with automatic parens
// turned off) and they also make it impossible to do balanced paren matching
// for macros, if that ever ends up being a thing.
//
// One option is to use colon somehow, sort of like MatLab... not sure how that
// helps with the inclusive/exclusive thing.
// a<:<b
// a=:<b
// a<:=b
// a=:=b
//
// Another question is how to implement a step size, if at all.
//
// And one other thing to keep in mind is slice syntax. For example, it would
// be kind of nice if a[3..] was "all the elements in a from 3 onwards."
// I think these also may need to be a different type.
//
// In terms of types, I think you have:
//
// RangeEE[T] <..< (exclusive-exclusive)
// RangeEI[T] <..= (exclusive-inclusive)
// RangeIE[T] =..<
// RangeII[T] =..=
// RangeEU[T] <..  (exclusive-unbounded)
// RangeUE[T]  ..<
// RangeUU[T]  ..
// RangeIU[T] =..
// RangeUI[T]  ..=
//
// which I guess is fine.
//
// 